#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153432 :tutorial/content "Consider the truth table for $S \\Rightarrow N$ below. Note that the implication is true $({\\color{blue}\\textrm{T}})$ in the $1$st, $3$rd, and $4$th rows. \n\n| $S$ | $N$ | $S \\Rightarrow N$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}\\textrm T$ | $\\color{red}\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue}\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue}\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue}\\textrm T$ |\n\nOf the three rows where the implication is true, $S$ is true $({\\color{red}\\textrm{T}})$ only in the $1$st row, and $N$ is also true $({\\color{red}\\textrm{T}})$ in this row. \n\nSo, if the implication is true, $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is called a **sufficient condition** for $N.$\n\nOn the other hand, if $S$ is true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is also true. Therefore, $N$ is called a **necessary condition** for $S.$ \nHowever, $N$ being true *does not guarantee* that $S$ is true, as we can see from the $3$rd row in the truth table.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\textbf{S}\\textrm{ufficient} \\: \\Rightarrow \\: \\textbf{N}\\textrm{ecessary}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nLet's look at some examples:\n\n- Consider the following true implication:\n\n$$\na > 0\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad a^2 > 0\n$$\n\nWe can label each part of the statement as follows:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\big( \\, a > 0 \\, \\big)}_{\\large\\textrm{sufficient}} \n\\: \\Rightarrow \\: \n\\underbrace{\\big( \\, a^2 > 0 \\, \\big)}_{\\large\\textrm{necessary}}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- $a > 0$ is a *sufficient* condition for $a^2 > 0.$  In other words, $a > 0$ *guarantees* that $a^2 > 0.$\n\n- $a^2 > 0$ is a *necessary* condition for $a > 0.$ In other words, if $a > 0,$ it is necessary that $a^2 > 0.$ However, $a^2 > 0$ *does not guarantee* that $a > 0.$\n\n- Suppose your teacher promises that *if* you score at least $90$ on the exam, *then* you will receive an $A.$ We can express this promise as the following implication:\n![](images/1b/1b2bcf888775f0be1dfc6d7b85c546c2f4c535bc0f04a7dadaceb06a0ab8110a.svg)\nLet's assume the teacher is telling the truth, so the implication is true. We can label each part of our implication as follows:\n![](images/5b/5b0dccacbb0c14d3b7c9a7a69f1cddda6b3a464bb2ae86b95989798d931d71d3.svg)\nTherefore:\n\n- Scoring at least $90$ on the exam is a *sufficient* condition for getting an $A.$ In other words, scoring at least $90$ *guarantees* that you'll get an $A.$\n\n- Getting an $A$ is a *necessary* condition for scoring at least $90.$ In other words, if you score at least $90,$ it necessarily follows that you'll receive $A.$ \n\nHowever, getting an $A$ *does not guarantee* that you scored at least $90$ on the exam. For example, the teacher may have decided to award an $A$ despite a score smaller than 90 if the exam was particularly tough that year." :tutorial/id #uuid "3e91a89c-375e-5c19-8d50-7a5d47800579" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8001 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153444 :tutorial/content "Suppose that an equivalence $P \\Leftrightarrow Q$ is true. Then, we have the following terminology:\n\n- $P$ is a **necessary and sufficient condition** for $Q$\n\n- $Q$ is a **necessary and sufficient condition** for $P$\n\nFor example, consider the following two predicates:\n\n- $35 \\mid n$\n\n- $5 \\mid n$ and $7 \\mid n$\n\nThese are both necessary and sufficient conditions for each other. \n\nIndeed, recall that a number is divisible by $35$ *if and only if* it's divisible by both $5$ and $7.$ In other words, we have\n\n![](images/f8/f850149a190068a3acc1225f6d56a73faef368e45ef69c1878eb933f94bc2f2b.svg)\n\nand\n\n![](images/7e/7eddbd9e552b236b3beca479b9f1e1d702709797dd087fe5d365af5ddac19ef2.svg)\n\nTherefore, we can write\n\n![](images/e6/e6d8d5fa95eee18645f6c7fed335d31cc4816900b91cb8697662b5cdf106b0ba.svg)\n\nIn words:\n\n- $\\big( 35 \\mid n \\big)$ *is necessary and sufficient for* ![](images/de/de6cda8624f7ab07fab89d309b47cd0b8908aa5c04a9f5cb142add5f2403ab6a.svg)\n\n- ![](images/0e/0e2189b2579fb028fb41d1eeb58494cdb591315f6b0ac0bae72ed848479b879a.svg) *is necessary and sufficient for* $\\big( 35 \\mid n \\big).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "87478f3c-00be-545c-b9ce-1618a66bf0b4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8321 :tutorial/title "Necessary and Sufficient Conditions"}]}
